WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
welcome to the newshour u.s president  joe biden has laid out his strategy for  the middle east at a summit of gulf  leaders and other regional heads of  state addressing the meeting in saudi  arabia biden said the u.s would not walk  away from the region and he said his  country remains committed to ensuring  iran never gets a nuclear weapon  let me state clearly  that the united states is going to  remain an active engaged partner in the  middle east  as the world grows more competitive  and the challenges we face more complex  is only becoming clearer to me  that how closely interwoven america's  interests are  with the successes of the middle east  we will not walk away  and leave a vacuum to be filled by  china russia or iran  will seek to build on this moment with  active principle american leadership  we call upon iran as a neighboring  country with whom we share religious and  cultural ties to cooperate with the  countries of the region in order to be  part of this vision by adhering to  international law not interfering in the  internal affairs of other countries and  cooperating with the international  atomic energy agency and fulfilling its  obligations in this regard  well joining me now here in the studio  is al jazeera senior political analyst  marwan bashar marwan can we start with  what joe biden's been saying why do you  think he felt the need to say that the  u.s was not going to abandon the region  because there's been a sense at least  since the u.s withdrew from afghanistan  and some of the administration officials  have been making clear that they're  going to be focusing more on russia and  china rather than  on deploying troops to the middle east  that created a lot of anxiety  among many of america's allies notably  those sitting there in the summit  and hence it was important for the  american president to make clear that  america is not going anywhere if  anything  america is truly is true is  reprioritizing  but if anything  withdrawing from afghanistan will free  more resources  for the gulf region in the middle east  let's not forget of course what uh biden  has called saudi arabia in the past he's  called it a pariah state nevertheless he  has gone there at a time in which the  u.s is looking to  shore up its supplies of energy  absolutely so i think there is a  national interest here  and uh biden is not hiding it he's  making it very clear that he's there to  serve american national interest even if  it is at the expense at the expense  of uh human rights uh whether it's human  rights of saudis uh egyptians  uh palestinians or whoever but be that  as it may america comes first america is  first and hence  he is eager to  uh reassert america's influence in the  region  uh reshape old alliances with the likes  of saudi arabia uae egypt iraq and so on  and so forth and make clear that america  is re-prioritizing but it's one going  anywhere my fear is while he's getting  some of his  mission accomplished  what we're what would what we're seeing  slowly but surely is  basically the evolving of a cold war in  the middle east with iran russia and  china on the one hand the united states  saudi arabia abu dhabi egypt and israel  on the other hand the one thing that  also has dominated this summit uh in in  jeddah also are  um  to do with the palestinian situation  without a doubt it's the it's the  elephant in the room it's what unites  all the gcc leaders along with their  regional partners um but of course you  know shortly after biden left uh israel  there were these rocket attacks from  gaza  what sort of tone do you think biden's  presence in the middle east has set what  sort of  problems has it reignited  look i i think uh and during the summit  um  we've seen the emir of qatar probably  articulate the best possible position  about double standard  and he's basically spoke about  international law international norms  and international values  and he said we need to be consistent  about the applications of these  so-called international values laws  and  and norms and it couldn't be simply  applied somewhere but not applied  elsewhere it couldn't be applied in  ukraine but not applied in palestine so  palestine really came to life by all  speakers in the summit uh today but i  think the amir of qatar put it very  clearly when he said  basically to the american president  we can't accept israel expanding illegal  jewish settlements instead of  advancing  peace settlement  in palestine and i think the message to  a lot of the presence there were you  know the abrahamic accords  in the end of the day they are not used  as leverage against israel in order to  pressure it to bring a solution to  palestine if anything  it is leaving the palestinians with very  little hope  that  their occupation will be ending anytime  soon all right bashar al jazeera senior  political analyst thank you  well joining me here from our dog our  headquarters is our white house  correspondent kimberly halcott staying  with this story kimberly i mean  obviously an awful lot has been made of  this fist pump between uh joe biden and  mohammed bin salman but beyond that do  you think the president got what he  wanted out of this trip  yeah that's right a lot has been made  about that fist bump and that is going  to be a problem likely for the u.s  president when he returns to the united  states because it was not well received  in fact the u.s president ended up  having to do a little bit of damage  control as a result  saying in fact that he confronted the  crown prince about  the allegations the  suggestion even by the cia that  potentially he ordered the killing of  the washington post journalist jamal  khashoggi back in 2018  and as a result there was a lot of  condemnation about that fist bump  especially from the washington post  publisher  given the fact that jamal khashoggi  wrote for that well-known paper  so moving forward from that was a  challenge for the president but still he  has tried to do that tres marwin is  talking about there reasserting the  united states role  in the middle east in the region is the  priority for this president but there's  no question that those negative  headlines are going to dog the president  for some time in fact uh we've even had  in just the last couple of hours the uh  sort of headlines of the crown prince  himself kind of taking a swipe at the  president  in fact suggesting that the president  really doesn't have the moral high  ground reminding the president of some  of the united states own mistakes as he  calls them reminding and digging up the  sort of past uh sort of bad headlines  for the u.s with respect to the conflict  in iraq the u.s invasion of iraq and of  course the atrocities the u.s committed  its military prison in abu ghraib and  then more recently of course the killing  of our own journalist sharina abu akley  the fact that the u.s president at least  in public did not confront the israeli  government over that murder despite the  fact that  or killing rather despite the fact that  the two investigations point to the  israelis for potentially being  responsible for her death so uh this is  uncomfortable for the u.s president he  now has to go back  he said he came here to get the saudi  government to pump more oil it hasn't  secured that deal and he hasn't been  able to lower the gas prices that  americans would like him to do so  it's going to be difficult for uh the  president right yeah and kimberly on  that note i mean how might all of this  play into domestic politics back home in  the states ahead of the midterms in  november  yeah that's the big problem for the  president because americans are pretty  unhappy with joe biden and they were  unhappy before he made this trip and he  didn't do anything to make it better uh  so it's hard to imagine that his poll  numbers are going to go up and they were  in need of that uh you you compare him  to donald trump and another sort of  unpopular president and the approval  ratings are about the same not much  better in fact in the 30s and that's the  sort of the benchmark of really  bottoming out in the united states and  that's not where you want to be when you  have congressional elections in november  there's not a whole lot of time to turn  things around joe biden needed a win he  didn't get one in terms of the foreign  policy stage and so  he goes back to the united states now  looking absent when americans are  struggling with high inflation high fuel  costs high food prices they're angry at  their president and this president did  nothing to improve that  kimberly many thanks kimberly halcott  our white house correspondent here in  doha forest thanks

